The convex front is a property of linear scalarization, not of the method. Stated for linear weights, the theorem recovers the part of the front reached by supporting hyperplanes, which is classical and holds for any linear-weight sweep. Steering itself works with any scalarization. Theorem 1 never uses linearity in 𝒘 { m{w}} , so it applies to any smooth scalarization. Non-smooth ones such as Tchebycheff are handled by the same smoothing argument used for the proxies. The softmin Tchebycheff of Eq. ( 14 ) is smooth and lies within ν ​ log ⁡ m 
u\log m of the Tchebycheff value everywhere, so Theorem 1 carries over up to that error. Empirically, all four scalarizations of Appendix B.2.6 ascend and reach the same front quality. The precomputation also carries over unchanged, because the cached per-objective gradients { ∇ ϕ a ​ ( 𝒛 i ) } \{
